title: How queries are evaluated
tags: [[Query engine]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: One evaluator over the store's bitmaps, no SQL and no fallback; two leaves are rewritten against the forest first, the cursor bound to the query sent; a naive oracle checks it.

A query is ''not'' compiled to SQL. Every operand is answered from the store's derived
key spaces — bitmaps of dense ids, value partitions, the forest's positions — and rows
are read only for the metarecords that come out ([[Store tables]]). There is one
engine (`index::Eval`, generic over a `Source`; `KvSource` is the one source) and
''no fallback'': a shape it cannot serve is a daemon bug, answered `500`, not a slow
answer; a cursor it did not issue is a `400`.

The steps of one request (`routes::run_query_filter`), each a phase of the slow log
([[What the slow log records]]):

# ''Validate'' — every `400` ([[Query validation]]).
# ''Prepare'' — resolve what goes through the forest: path targets of `->`/`->*`/`=>*` and exact-node operands of `=`/`!=` become node uuids handed to the evaluator (`QueryRoots`); the two leaves no bitmap can hold — a `:path` predicate and an order-sensitive `osm` path — are resolved against the forest and ''rewritten'' into the `uuid_in` set they match ([[The forest as a query provider]]).
# ''Evaluate'' — the filter as bitmap operations; the scans (`MATCHES`, `osmd`, `!=`, ranges) run last inside an `AND`, on the running intersection, checking a few candidates on their own rows ([[Query planner]]); a subtree is a descendant bitmap; `count` is the bitmap's cardinality.
# ''Page'' — fetch or walk, by estimated cost ([[Sorting and postings]]); a page without a count checks text predicates lazily, only where the sort looks ([[Text search in the store]]).

Each request reads one snapshot of the store; nothing is resident between requests.

!! The cursor is bound to the query sent

A page's cursor is bound to a hash of the query ''as the client sent it'', not as
rewritten ([[Pagination and sorting]]). The rewrite runs again for every page and
answers with the sets that match ''now'': bound to it, a cursor died whenever that set
changed — a file arriving while the finder paged a two-word search answered page 2
with `400 invalid cursor` (November 2026,
`tests/query_http.rs::test_a_rewritten_query_s_cursor_survives_a_new_match`). Before
that, the same `400` came from a rewrite returning its set in another order: three of
the four exits of the `osm` path resolution built their answer in a `HashSet`.

The rewrite is still deterministic, and held to it by "two calls, same output"
(`tests/index_oracle.rs::resolving_forest_leaves_is_deterministic`): a set in a
stable order keeps the evaluation reproducible, though the cursor no longer depends on
it. The evaluator takes the client's query as `QueryRoots::cursor_query`.

!! The oracle

Every shape is held to a naive second evaluator that reads rows only:
[[The query oracle]].
